  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 131 AM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nBy Sunday and into next week, troughing to the north and west helps \npush the upper-level ridge east of our area. This causes \ntemperatures to steadily decrease through next week. On Sunday, \nhighs remain in the upper 90s and just over 100 degrees for most \nlocations. By Wednesday and Thursday highs in the low-to-mid 90s \nwill have become more commonplace. It should be noted, however, that \nthese temperatures will still be about 5-10 degrees above normal for \nthis time of year (for reference, 87 degrees is the average high at \nKMAF for September 16th and 17th). Decreasing subsidence and nearby \npulses in the flow aloft will also help encourage increasing rain \nchances next week, mainly for the western half of the area. For \nSunday, rain chances will mainly exist in/around the higher terrain \nwest of the Pecos (10-20%). From Monday through the end of the \nforecast period, chances for southeast New Mexico and locations \nsouth and west of the Pecos River will generally be in the 20-50% \nrange. \n\nSprang",
